# Updates, repairs and maintenance

Maintenance information should explain the affected software or device, the available action and its consequences. Keep it distinct from immediate household hazards. A notification count is a route to maintenance detail, not evidence that a problem has been fixed.

## Scope and native ownership

Native update entities expose installed/latest/skipped versions and may support installation and progress. An off state can include a skipped release. Native Repairs supplies issue-specific information or repair steps; civica changes badge presentation, not repair state. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/update/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-020**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Update available | Show installed and offered versions with release information. | Do not infer severity from a version number. |
| Skipped release | Name the skipped version when supplied. | Do not claim the latest version is installed. |
| Downloading / installing | Use only stages and progress the provider exposes. | Do not add a simulated percentage or cancellation promise. |
| Restart required | Explain the affected service and expected interruption if known. | Do not restart when opening the card. |
| Repair dismissed / unresolved | Keep acknowledgement distinct from remedy. | Removing a badge does not prove resolution. |

## Integration contract

1. Use the native permission and update/repair workflow. Installation capability, backup support and cancellation vary by integration.
2. Show the actual software title as well as any household-friendly device name. Preserve release identifiers exactly and identify external release-note links.
3. Before installing, state known interruptions and prerequisites from the actual provider. Do not invent downtime guarantees or assume every device can roll back.
4. Keep numeric attention counts owned by Home Assistant. The MOJ-derived civica badge does not create, deliver, dismiss or resolve maintenance records.

## Accessible interaction

- Keep release details readable before the primary action. A version comparison belongs in text, not only coloured tags.
- Announce stage transitions once; preserve focus and native repair-dialog names through theme changes.
- Make failed and skipped states visible without requiring colour discrimination or opening a hover tooltip.

## Failure and recovery

- If installation is rejected, retain the installed version and provider explanation; never change the version optimistically.
- If progress stops reporting, say progress unavailable and inspect the native operation before retrying.
- If a repair only supplies instructions, show that route rather than an invented Fix now button.

